<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#alanic-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alanic language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alanic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alanic was the Eastern Iranian language of the Alans, a nomadic people of the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in late antiquity and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. It is known only from names, a few words and short texts, among them Alan phrases recorded by the twelfth-century Byzantine writer John Tzetzes. Modern Ossetian, spoken in North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-ossetia">South Ossetia</a>, is its direct descendant.</p></div>
